          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01HimAiavaszDYtxsytEocTo",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/bloom-filter.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Bloom Filter\\ndescription: A probabilistic set-membership data structure that answers membership queries with tunable false-positive rates.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-06T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/data-structures\\n  - concept/probabilistic-algorithms\\n  - tech/algorithms\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single academic/technical source covering basic definition and properties; well-established concept but no corroboration in this wiki yet.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nA bloom filter is a probabilistic set-membership data structure that efficiently tests whether an element is present in a set. Unlike deterministic set representations, it makes a probabilistic trade-off between space efficiency and query accuracy.\\n\\n## Characteristics\\n\\n**Query Results:**\\n- **\\u0022Definitely not present\\u0022** \\u2014 guaranteed accurate. If the filter says an element is not in the set, it is certainly not present.\\n- **\\u0022Possibly present\\u0022** \\u2014 has a tunable false-positive rate. If the filter says an element is present, it might be (true positive) or might not be (false positive).\\n\\n**Implementation:**\\n- Uses a bit array of a fixed size.\\n- Employs k independent hash functions.\\n- Each hash function maps an element to a position in the bit array.\\n- Adding an element: set the bits at all k hash positions to 1.\\n- Testing membership: check if all k corresponding positions are set to 1.\\n\\n## Limitations\\n\\n**No Enumeration:**\\nBloom filters cannot enumerate the members they contain \\u2014 there is no way to iterate over all elements that were added. The bit array does not preserve element identity.\\n\\n**No Deletion (Basic Form):**\\nThe basic bloom filter does not support deletion. Removing an element would require unsetting one or more bits, but this could break membership queries for other elements whose hash values overlap with the deleted element\\u0027s bit positions.\\n\\n## Trade-offs\\n\\nThe structure prioritizes:\\n- **Space efficiency** \\u2014 much smaller than storing actual set members\\n- **Query speed** \\u2014 O(k) lookups where k is typically a small constant\\n\\nAt the cost of:\\n- False positives (controlled by the false-positive rate parameter)\\n- No enumeration or deletion\\n\\n## Applications\\n\\nBloom filters are commonly used in:\\n- Membership testing where false positives are tolerable and space is constrained\\n- Caching and cache invalidation\\n- Duplicate detection in large datasets\\n- P2P networks and databases\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: General definition and properties of bloom filters as a probabilistic set-membership data structure with configurable false-positive rates.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
